The pills keep their Norwegian names because they are the literal tags in the wiki; translating them would make the diagram lie. The colours are the ones on the stripe at the top of each tiddler, read from [[kind-colours|$:/plugins/intertwingled-innovations/nhn/kind-colours]]. A ''dashed'' pill is a tag with no tiddler behind it, which is worth looking at: `Resultat`, `mal`, `Arkiv` and the year tags are used freely without anyone having created the tiddler, while `Ekstern tjeneste` is dashed because only the spelling `Ekstern Tjeneste` has one — the same drift [[Anomalier]] cleans up.

!! What the arrows mean

Every link runs one way: //the child tags the parent's title//. There are no foreign-key fields. `TjenesteID` looks like a key but is not one — it is on most of the reviews and only sporadically elsewhere, so it cannot join deliveries or OKRs to a service. Use the tag.

`service-name tag` is the one join all content shares: a review, an objective, a delivery or a task belongs to a service if it is tagged with that service's title.

!! Where the model breaks down in practice

* 22% of the reviews have no service-name tag and 84% no `Forretningsmessig endring`. [[ToDo forretningsgjennomgang]] compensates by reading the service name out of the title when the tag is missing; [[ToDo målsettinger]] does not, because objective titles follow no convention.
* The tags drift in spelling — `Ekstern Tjeneste` against `Ekstern tjeneste`, `Mars` against `mars`. [[Anomalier]] derives the families and offers to merge them.
* Drafts inherit the tags of what they draft, so an unfinished copy counts as real content unless it is subtracted.

The details, with counts from the data, are in `docs/data-model.md` in the repo.

<% else %>

! Datamodell

Hvordan tagger og felter henger sammen. Taggene //er// skjemaet — det finnes ingen egne datatyper, bare vanlige tiddlere med tagger og felter. Klikk en node for å åpne tiddleren eller taggen den står for.

